{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n58 \n \n \nAppendix F. ISS Operations per the Payload Weekly Science Summary \n \n2019 \nFirst round (normal flames) \nIncrement 59/60 \nWeek 5: April 5-11 \nThis week, Christina Koch configured the ACME Chamber Insert to support the next ACME experiment, \nFlame Design. Also to support Flame Design, Oleg Kononenko replaced the bottles on CIR Manifold #2, \nCIR Manifold #4, and the CIR GC Helium Manifold. Flame Design is focused on understanding the soot \nprocesses of flames. Test points will start in a few weeks. \n \nThe April 11 reporting on the completion of BRE\u2019s first round was not included here.  \n \nWeek 8: April 26 \u2013 May 2 \nOn GMT 115, the first ACME Flame Design test points were performed. Pure gaseous ethylene fuel was \nejected into air from a porous sphere, which, in microgravity, results in a one-dimensional, spherical \nflame. Thus, data analysis is greatly simplified because the flame shape doesn\u2019t need to be predicted, and \ncomputational modeling can focus on the detailed chemistry and transport of the molecules and energy. \n \nThe goal of Flame Design is to improve our understanding of soot processes so that flames can be \n\u201cdesigned\u201d to be soot free. This research could help lead to reduced soot and NOx\u2014and thus reduced \nsmog and acid rain\u2014through nitrogen exchange, where the nitrogen is shifted from the air to the fuel. \nThis oxygen-enriched combustion of diluted fuel could also facilitate carbon sequestration (i.e., capture \nand storage). \n \n \nFigure 1. Flame Design Flame (Example Preliminary Monochrome Image)", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 58}}